/// A constructor may be named at the call.
///
/// Once the keyword decides rather than the name, a class may declare several
/// constructors with different names -- and two of them can take the same
/// signature, which calling the bare class name cannot distinguish.  Naming one
/// is how a program says which it means.
///
/// ⚠️ This is what makes named constructors worth having at all.  Circle(5.0)
/// and Circle.FromDiameter(10.0) both take one Double, so overload resolution
/// has nothing to separate them; only the name does.  Where the signatures DO
/// differ, the bare form still works and naming is optional.
///
/// Turbo Pascal names the constructor at every call -- New(P, Init) -- because
/// its objects are allocated rather than expressed.  Here the bare form stays
/// the common case and naming is the escape hatch.
///
/// Fails with: Only instances have properties.  A class is not an instance and
/// cannot answer a dot at all today, so naming a constructor fails one step
/// earlier than a missing member would.
///
class Circle;
var
    Radius : Double := 0.0;

begin
    constructor Init (Radius : Double);
    begin
        this.Radius := Radius;
    end

    constructor FromDiameter (Across : Double);
    begin
        this.Radius := Across / 2.0;
    end
end

test 'A constructor may be named at the call';
begin
    AssertEqual (5.0, Circle (5.0).Radius);
    AssertEqual (5.0, Circle.FromDiameter (10.0).Radius);
end
